// Practice quiz: A dev profile that keeps endpoints open during front-end work
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/api-security/a-dev-profile-that-keeps-endpoints-open-during-front-end-work

::o1 profile bean::What does @Profile("dev") on a bean method do? {
=The bean exists only when the dev profile is active#Correct. Otherwise, it is not created.
~It logs at DEBUG#Not quite. It is unrelated.
~It disables Spring Security everywhere#Not quite. It affects only this bean.
~It renames the bean#Not quite. It does not rename it.
}

::o1 cors::Why does a front end on port 5173 calling an API on port 8080 need CORS? {
=Different ports make a different origin#Correct. The browser enforces this.
~Java blocks it#Not quite. The browser is what enforces CORS.
~Port 5173 is reserved#Not quite. It is not.
~JSON requires it#Not quite. It is unrelated to JSON.
}

::o2 proving::How do you prove that the default profile still enforces security? {
=Run security tests without the dev profile and assert 401#Correct. The test fails if the default opens up.
~Read the configuration once#Not quite. That does not stop later changes.
~Run only with dev active#Not quite. That tests the open chain instead.
~Check the port#Not quite. A port says nothing about rules.
}

::o3 risk::What is the danger of spring.profiles.active\=dev in application.properties? {
=It ships in the jar, so every environment may start open#Correct. Packaged defaults reach production.
~Nothing changes#Not quite. The profile would be active.
~It only affects tests#Not quite. It affects any run.
~It deletes data#Not quite. It is about access.
}

::o3 guard::What is a useful last line of defense against an open profile in production? {
=A startup check that refuses to start#Correct. It catches the mistake immediately.
~A longer log message#Not quite. It would not stop anything.
~A wiki page#Not quite. It relies on memory.
~A bigger heap#Not quite. It is unrelated.
}

